@using AniQueue.Core.Artwork

@*
    A title's art, at whatever fidelity is available.

    Three states in a fixed order, because they degrade into one another rather than
    being alternatives: the cached picture, then the dominant colour AniList
    published, then nothing at all. The colour covers 92% of titles and is what a
    first run shows for the few minutes before the pictures land — which is the whole
    reason the colour is stored at all.

    The image is decorative, deliberately. The title is in the cell beside it, so an
    alt text would be the same words announced twice; a screen reader should hear a
    row once. Explicit dimensions and lazy loading are what keep a fifty-row page from
    reflowing under itself as pictures arrive.
*@

@* The quiet class is for having nothing at all, not for not having a picture yet.
   A colour block is a deliberate rendering of a real fact the source published and
   should look like one; only a title with neither is a gap. *@
@if (OnActivate.HasDelegate)
{
    @* A pointer convenience and nothing more. Clicking a poster to open a
       title is what every media library does, so it is worth having — but it is a
       second way to reach a control the row already has on its title, and announcing
       both would make a screen reader read every row twice. So this stays hidden and
       unreachable by keyboard, and the title carries the real, named button.

       It is on every card at every width, where the picture is the
       leading element. That does not
       promote it: it is still a shortcut to what the title does, and still silent. *@
    <button type="button"
            class="cover-thumb cover-thumb-button @(Cover.IsEmpty ? "cover-thumb-empty" : null)"
            style="@ColourStyle()"
            tabindex="-1"
            aria-hidden="true"
            @onclick="OnActivate">
        @if (Cover.HasImage)
        {
            @* The attributes state the shape rather than the size. CSS sets how big
               the slot is; these give the browser the 2:3 of a poster to reserve
               before the picture lands, which is what stops a page of fifty cards
               shuffling under itself as they arrive. *@
            <img src="@Cover.Url" alt="" width="2" height="3" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        }
    </button>
}
else
{
    <span class="cover-thumb @(Cover.IsEmpty ? "cover-thumb-empty" : null)"
          style="@ColourStyle()"
          aria-hidden="true">
        @if (Cover.HasImage)
        {
            <img src="@Cover.Url" alt="" width="2" height="3" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        }
    </span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>What to render, already resolved. This component decides nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public CoverArt Cover { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What to do when the poster is clicked, where clicking it does anything.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Unset on a surface with nothing to open, which is what keeps this decorative
    /// in the places it always was. Set, it becomes a pointer shortcut to a control
    /// that also exists on the row's title — never the only way to reach it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnActivate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The colour as a custom property rather than a background, so the stylesheet
    /// owns how it is used — a flat fill today, and whatever a later layout wants
    /// without this component changing. Null when the source published no colour, or
    /// published one that is not six hexadecimal digits, which the resolver rejects
    /// before it can reach a style attribute at all.
    /// </summary>
    private string? ColourStyle() =>
        Cover.Colour is { } colour ? $"--cover-colour: {colour}" : null;
}
